DAY 37 - PART A: THEORY
Scheduler and Controller Manager: Reconciliation Loops
Time: 40 minutes
==================================================================

1. THE CONTROLLER PATTERN
-------------------------
A controller is a loop:
  for ever:
      desired = read spec from API (via a watch + local cache)
      actual  = observe the world (pods, nodes, cloud resources)
      if different: take ONE step toward desired, write status
It is LEVEL-triggered: it reacts to the current state, not to a list
of past events. If it crashes and restarts, it just looks again.


2. CONTROLLERS INSIDE kube-controller-manager
---------------------------------------------
  Deployment controller     Deployment -> ReplicaSets
  ReplicaSet controller     ReplicaSet -> Pods
  StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job, CronJob controllers
  Node controller           marks nodes NotReady/Unknown, taints them,
                            triggers eviction after timeout
  EndpointSlice controller  Service + ready pods -> EndpointSlices
  ServiceAccount, Namespace (deletes contents), PV binder,
  Garbage collector         deletes objects whose owner is gone
                            (ownerReferences, cascading delete)
  ResourceQuota, HPA (Week 7), CertificateSigningRequest ...


3. A CHAIN OF CONTROLLERS (k create deploy web --replicas=2)
-----------------------------------------------------------
  you  -> Deployment saved
  Deployment controller  -> creates ReplicaSet
  ReplicaSet controller  -> creates 2 Pods (no node)
  Scheduler              -> sets nodeName on each
  kubelet                -> runs containers, sets status
  EndpointSlice ctrl     -> adds ready pod IPs to Services
Each step is a separate controller reacting to the previous one's
output. Nobody orchestrates the whole flow.


4. THE SCHEDULER IN DETAIL
--------------------------
Scheduling framework, plugins at each stage:
  PreFilter/Filter  NodeResourcesFit, NodeAffinity, TaintToleration,
                    PodTopologySpread, VolumeBinding, NodePorts ...
  Score             NodeResourcesBalancedAllocation, ImageLocality,
                    InterPodAffinity, TaintToleration ...
  Bind              write the Binding
If no node fits: pod stays Pending, event "FailedScheduling" with a
summary like "0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated
taint ..., 2 Insufficient cpu". Preemption may evict lower-priority
pods to make room.

You can run several schedulers; a pod chooses with spec.schedulerName.


5. OWNER REFERENCES AND GARBAGE COLLECTION
------------------------------------------
Pod.metadata.ownerReferences -> ReplicaSet -> Deployment.
Deleting the Deployment:
  --cascade=background (default)  owner deleted, GC removes children
  --cascade=foreground            children first, then owner
  --cascade=orphan                children left alone


6. NODE FAILURE TIMELINE (defaults)
-----------------------------------
  kubelet stops heartbeating (Lease renew every 10 s)
  ~40 s  node controller marks node NotReady/Unknown, adds
         node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute taint
  +300 s pods' default toleration expires -> pods evicted ->
         controllers create replacements on healthy nodes
So a dead node's pods move after ~5-6 minutes by default (tunable via
tolerationSeconds per pod).


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Controllers are level-triggered loops that close the gap.
- Work happens through a chain of independent controllers.
- FailedScheduling events explain Pending pods.
- Dead node -> pods evicted after ~5 minutes.
